From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Meir Dick <mdick85@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 09:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tas2783: do not fail runtime resume on a late
 peripheral

tas2783_sdca_dev_resume() waits up to five seconds for the peripheral
to finish re-enumerating after a bus reset and fails the resume if it
does not. On the HP EliteBook X G2i one of the four amplifiers on the
link (uid 0x9) can take more than ten seconds to come back. Every PCM
open on the card goes through this runtime resume, so while that one
part is late the open fails, WirePlumber's probe fails with it, and the
card is left with no usable profile (device names shortened to the
unique_id):

  uid 0x9: Initialization not complete
  uid 0x9: ASoC error (-22): at snd_soc_pcm_component_pm_runtime_get()
  $ speaker-test -D hw:0,2
  Playback open error: -22,Invalid argument

Seen on a second board of the same design (HP EliteBook X Flip G2i).

Treat runtime resume the way system resume already is: if the
peripheral has re-attached, restore the register cache; if it has not,
leave the regmap in cache-only mode and return. tas_update_status()
re-initializes the part when it arrives.

That alone is not enough, because the stream paths then fail for the
same part: .startup tries to re-initialize it over a bus it is not on
and returns -EIO, and hw_params refuses a part with no firmware. Every
codec DAI on the link has to succeed for the PCM to open, so one absent
amplifier still took the card down. Make both tolerate it: skip the
re-init when the part is not attached, and leave a part with no
firmware out of the stream instead of failing it. hw_free already
tolerates a part that was never added. The card keeps working on the
amplifiers that are present; the late one joins on the next stream
start after it attaches.

Report the outcome of the UEFI calibration read at info and warn level
instead of debug. Whether the factory calibration was found decides
whether a part runs with its own protection limits, and on this board
one amplifier is silent without it; that has to be visible in an
ordinary journal.

Baojun Xu's "ASoC: tas2783-sdw: add firmware download status check"
(v4, 2026-09-15, patchwork 14815978) works on the initialisation side
of the same problem and leaves the wait in the resume callback in
place, so nothing here overlaps it.

Signed-off-by: Meir Dick <mdick85@gmail.com>
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c | 62 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 53 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
index d69d80c..a09042b 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2783-sdw.c
@@ -738,7 +738,9 @@ static void tas2783_set_calib_params_to_device(struct tas2783_prv *tas_dev, u32
 		dev_err(tas_dev->dev,
 			"unique id not found in the calib data\n");
 	else
-		dev_dbg(tas_dev->dev, "calib data update done\n");
+		dev_info(tas_dev->dev,
+			 "factory calibration applied (%u speakers in UEFI)\n",
+			 dev_count);
 }
 
 static s32 tas2783_update_calibdata(struct tas2783_prv *tas_dev)
@@ -788,8 +790,13 @@ static s32 tas2783_update_calibdata(struct tas2783_prv *tas_dev)
 	}
 
 	if (status != EFI_SUCCESS) {
-		/* Failed got calibration data from EFI. */
-		dev_dbg(tas_dev->dev, "No calibration data in UEFI.");
+		/*
+		 * Failed to get calibration data from EFI. Say so where an
+		 * ordinary journal shows it: the part runs with the tuning's
+		 * default protection limits, and on some boards one amplifier
+		 * produces no output at all without its factory data.
+		 */
+		dev_warn(tas_dev->dev, "no factory calibration found in UEFI, running uncalibrated\n");
 		return 0;
 	}
 
@@ -1036,8 +1043,16 @@ static s32 tas_sdw_hw_params(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 	s32 ret, retry = 3;
 
 	if (!tas_dev->fw_dl_success) {
-		dev_err(tas_dev->dev, "error playback without fw download");
-		return -EINVAL;
+		/*
+		 * No firmware means the part is not on the bus or has not
+		 * finished coming back. Do not add it to the stream, and do
+		 * not fail the stream for it either: the amplifiers that are
+		 * present keep playing. hw_free tolerates a part that was
+		 * never added.
+		 */
+		dev_warn(tas_dev->dev,
+			 "no firmware loaded, leaving this amplifier out of the stream\n");
+		return 0;
 	}
 
 	sdw_stream = snd_soc_dai_get_dma_data(dai, substream);
@@ -1141,6 +1156,19 @@ static int tas_sdw_startup(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream,
 	if (!tas_dev->force_reinit && tas_dev->fw_dl_success)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * A part that is not on the bus cannot be re-initialized, and
+	 * failing the stream for it would take the other amplifiers on the
+	 * link down with it. Let the stream start without this one;
+	 * tas_update_status() initializes it when it attaches, and the next
+	 * stream picks it up.
+	 */
+	if (tas_dev->status != SDW_SLAVE_ATTACHED) {
+		dev_warn(tas_dev->dev,
+			 "not attached, starting the stream without it\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	tas_dev->hw_init = false;
 	regcache_cache_only(tas_dev->regmap, false);
 
@@ -1287,12 +1315,28 @@ static s32 tas2783_sdca_dev_resume(struct device *dev)
 	struct tas2783_prv *tas_dev = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = sdw_slave_wait_for_init(slave, TAS2783_PROBE_TIMEOUT);
-	if (ret) {
-		sdw_show_ping_status(slave->bus, true);
-		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * After a bus reset the peripheral has to re-enumerate and reload its
+	 * firmware, which tas_update_status() does in bus context. One of
+	 * the four amplifiers on the HP EliteBook X G2i (uid 0x9) can take
+	 * over ten seconds to come back. Waiting for it here, with the
+	 * five second timeout that used to be applied, made every PCM open
+	 * on the card fail with -ETIMEDOUT for as long as that one part was
+	 * late: WirePlumber probed the card, the open failed, and the whole
+	 * card was left with no usable profile.
+	 *
+	 * Do not wait. If the peripheral has re-attached, restore the cache
+	 * as before. If it has not, leave the regmap in cache-only mode and
+	 * let it be re-initialized when it arrives; the .startup re-init
+	 * brings a late part into a running stream. The card keeps working
+	 * on the amplifiers that are present in the meantime.
+	 */
+	if (!try_wait_for_completion(&slave->initialization_complete)) {
+		dev_dbg(dev, "runtime resume: not re-enumerated yet, deferring re-init\n");
+		return 0;
 	}
 
+	slave->unattach_request = 0;
 	regcache_cache_only(tas_dev->regmap, false);
 	ret = regcache_sync(tas_dev->regmap);
 	if (ret) {
